---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Maindeck Analysis - Replenish Guide"
    prev={{ name: "Introduction & History", href: "/" }}
    next={{ name: "Sideboard", href: "/sideboard" }}
>
    <h1 style="margin-top: 2rem; font-size: 3rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">
        Maindeck Analysis
    </h1>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">The Core</h2>

    <CardAnalysis title="Replenish (4)" image="sorceries/replenish.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Always a four-of. The one card whose count has never been
            challenged.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Opalescence (4)" image="enchantments/opalescence.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Turns all other non-Aura enchantments into creatures based on mana
            value. Opalescence does not affect itself—you need two for each to
            turn the other into a 4/4. With a single Opalescence, your attackers
            are Decree (8/8), Wave (4/4), Attunement (3/3), and Seals (2/2).
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis
        title="Parallax Wave (4)"
        image="enchantments/parallax_wave.webp"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Defensive early, combo piece late. With Opalescence, it permanently
            exiles every creature your opponent controls.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Attunement (4)" image="enchantments/attunement.jpg">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            This is the glue. Digs three cards deep, fills the graveyard, and
            attacks as a 3/3 under Opalescence. It's also the most commonly
            misplayed card in the deck. A lot of players just spin it hoping to
            find something good. Don't do that. Every activation should have a
            plan. Before you activate, you should roughly know which cards
            you're willing to discard. If you can't identify them, don't
            activate yet.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">The Lock Piece</h2>

    <CardAnalysis
        title="Decree of Silence (2–3)"
        image="enchantments/decree_of_silence.jpg"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            This replaced Parallax Tide as the post-Replenish lock and it's
            turned out to be much better than I expected. The full combo is
            great, but what surprised me is how good Decree is on its own. Just
            discarding it to Frantic Search or Attunement and Replenishing it
            back, even without the other pieces, can end games. Their hand gets
            too clunky to fight through three counters, and by the time the
            Decree is gone you've assembled everything else.
        </p>
        <p>
            Against control, cycling it for six mana is an uncounterable
            cantrip. Opponents are consistently surprised when you cycle it.
            They gave it to you off Intuition thinking who cares about an
            eight-mana enchantment, and then the uncounterable draw refuels you
            at a critical moment.
        </p>
        <p>Under Opalescence it's an 8/8, which is also not nothing.</p>
        <p>
            You want more copies when the field is diverse or combo-heavy.
            Against control specifically, where you're cutting Frantic Search
            and Intuition post-board, you can't reliably discard it, so the
            third copy can be a liability.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Proactive Disruption</h2>

    <CardAnalysis title="Meddling Mage" image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            I want four Mage total—whether they are main or side is metagaming.
            The move to maindeck happened preparing for a paper tournament: the
            shared clock made a proactive element desirable against Landstill.
            It also improves combo, Sligh, and Stiflenought matchups
            simultaneously. Maindeck Mage is a meta call—it excels in the
            metagames I play in, but it’s not required in the main.
        </p>
        <p>
            Naming is context-dependent. Against Sligh, name Lightning Bolt not
            to lock them out, but to protect Mage as a blocker for Jackal Pup.
            The Mage’s job against red is to eat a creature. Late game, shift to
            Fireblast or Ball Lightning. Against control: Counterspell or
            Swords. Against Stiflenought: Dreadnought or Stifle. Against
            Enchantress: Enchantress (Argothian Enchantress or Enchantress’s
            Precense) in game 1, Tormod’s Crypt in game 2.
        </p>
        <p>
            Main weakness: bad against green decks, discard, and midrange.
            Against Ponza, Rock, and Terrageddon; Mages come out because green
            creatures outclass a 2/2 and these decks don’t rely on single spells
            Mage can profitably name. In fields heavy with these archetypes,
            some or all Mages may belong in the sideboard.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Card Selection</h2>

    <CardAnalysis
        title="Frantic Search (3)"
        image="instants/frantic_search.webp"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Velocity. Makes explosive turns possible, especially with Sol lands.
            It's also the card I side out the most. In any matchup where the
            game goes long and card disadvantage hurts, it comes out.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Intuition (3)" image="instants/intuition.webp">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            The most skill-testing card in the deck. Classic pile is
            Opalescence, Wave, Decree. Whatever they give you, the other two go
            to the graveyard for Replenish. But the piles change based on what
            you're playing against. Against Landstill you might pile three
            lands. Against aggro, three Swords. I often trim it against control
            because it lowers your threat density.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;">Interaction</h2>

    <CardAnalysis
        title="Swords to Plowshares (3 main, 1 side)"
        image="instants/swords_to_plowshares.webp"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Essential one-mana removal. The fourth comes in against
            creature-heavy matchups.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis
        title="Seal of Cleansing (2-3 main, 1 side)"
        image="enchantments/seal_of_cleansing.webp"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Answers problematic enchantments and artifacts. It synergizes with
            Replenish since it comes back from the graveyard ready to use again.
            Comes out against decks that don't have relevant targets.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis
        title="Tsabo’s Web (1-3 main, 1 side)"
        image="artifacts/tsabos_web.jpg"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Shuts down Port, Dust Bowl, Factory. The count flexes from one to
            three depending on how many utility lands you expect. It's been
            trending down online as Terrageddon has declined.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <h2
        style="margin-top: 4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-50); padding-top: 2rem;"
    >
        No Maindeck Mana Leak
    </h2>
    <CardAnalysis title="Mana Leak" image="instants/mana_leak.webp">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            I cut Mana Leak because Landstill was the most popular control deck
            when I was building the list. Leak is bad in long games where they
            can just pay the three. With Landstill declining and Esper Psychatog
            picking up, Leak looks better now. The mirror is also a real matchup
            post-ban, which makes cheap countermagic more valuable. For now I've
            been using Defense Grid from the sideboard to handle blue matchups,
            but Mana Leak is worth reconsidering depending on your field.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <h2
        style="margin-top: 4rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-50); padding-top: 2rem;"
    >
        Other Cards Worth Considering
    </h2>

    <CardAnalysis title="Deep Analysis" image="sorceries/deep_analysis.webp">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Not in the current list, but it's been in prior versions and I could
            see it coming back. The appeal is that it gives you a card advantage
            option for Intuition piles in matchups where velocity doesn't
            matter. Against control specifically, you don't want to Intuition
            for combo pieces because it lowers your threat density, but
            Intuition for Deep Analysis is actually useful. If your meta has a
            lot of Landstill or other slow blue decks, one copy maindeck is
            worth considering.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis title="Fact or Fiction" image="instants/fact_or_fiction.webp">
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            This was more popular when the deck ran Mana Leak and had more of a
            reactive, play-on-your-opponent's-turn shell. The four mana cost is
            the downside, but if you're already on Mana Leaks then Fact or
            Fiction makes a lot of sense. It's card advantage, it digs five
            deep, and it puts your opponent in difficult spots on how to split
            the piles. If the meta shifts back toward a more interactive build
            with countermagic, I'd consider one or two.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>


    <h2
        style="margin-top: 4rem; font-size: 2.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-50); padding-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-bottom: 2rem;"
    >
        Mana Base Theory
    </h2>
    <p>
        <strong>25 Lands</strong><br />I've tried 24 and 26. Twenty-five feels
        right, though I wouldn’t fault anyone for 26.
    </p>
    <div
        style="display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; align-items: flex-start; flex-wrap: wrap;"
    >
        <div style="display: flex; gap: 1rem; flex-shrink: 0; flex-wrap: wrap;">
            <img
                src="/images/cards/lands/island.jpg"
                alt="Island"
                style="width: 220px; border-radius: 4.75% / 3.5%; border: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-50);"
            />
            <img
                src="/images/cards/lands/plains.jpg"
                alt="Plains"
                style="width: 220px; border-radius: 4.75% / 3.5%; border: 1px solid var(--w-color-primary-50);"
            />
        </div>
        <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 300px;">
            <h3 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                Ten Basics (5 Island, 5 Plains)
            </h3>
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Five Islands, five Plains. This makes you resilient against
                Price of Progress and Wasteland. Starting with your first three
                land drops as basics is a realistic line against red and mana
                denial strategies. It meets Karsten's threshold of roughly 16
                sources for each color to cast double-colored four-drops on
                time.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <CardAnalysis
        title="Skycloud Expanse (3)"
        image="lands/skycloud_expanse.webp"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Down from four for the fifth basic of each type.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <CardAnalysis
        title="City of Traitors (0)"
        image="lands/city_of_traitors.webp"
    >
        <p style="margin-top: 0;">
            Without Tide’s lock, games go longer. City disrupts sequencing and
            loses value in attrition matchups.
        </p>
    </CardAnalysis>

    <p>
        <strong>Turn-One Sequencing</strong><br />If I have a dual and a basic,
        I'll usually lead with the dual to keep options open for my second draw.
        Against Wasteland decks, I lead basic instead. It depends on the hand.
    </p>
</Layout>
